[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence needed. The fault is completely clear and localized.

**Summary of findings:**
- laptop0: `default via 172.16.20.99 dev eth0` — the gateway IP 172.16.20.99 does not exist on any device in the network.
- router0 eth0.20 = 172.16.20.1/24 — the **real** VLAN 20 gateway is `.1`, not `.99`.
- laptop0 pings 172.16.20.1 successfully (L2 path is intact); 172.16.20.99 is "Destination Host Unreachable".
- VLAN 10 hosts (pc4 etc.) all use 172.16.10.1 (router0 eth0.10) — correct and working.
- mswitch1 br_vlan20 has no IP at all — consistent with router0 being the sole gateway.

---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
Laptop0's default gateway is misconfigured to 172.16.20.99, an IP address that
does not exist on any device in the network. The correct VLAN 20 inter-VLAN
routing gateway is 172.16.20.1, which lives on router0's subinterface eth0.20.
Because 172.16.20.99 is phantom, every ARP request laptop0 sends for its
gateway goes unanswered (kernel reports "FAILED" for that neighbour), so all
traffic with a destination outside 172.16.20.0/24 is silently dropped at
laptop0 itself — the packet never leaves the host. Intra-VLAN 20 traffic (e.g.
to other VLAN 20 peers) works because those destinations are on-link and bypass
the default route entirely. VLAN 10 is unaffected because pc4/pc5/laptop2/laptop3
all point to 172.16.10.1, which is router0's eth0.10 subinterface and is fully
reachable. Direct ping from laptop0 to 172.16.20.1 succeeds (round-trip ~1 ms),
proving the L2 path through switch1 → mswitch1 → router0 is intact and that
only the gateway pointer is wrong. Root cause: laptop0's startup configuration
sets its default route via 172.16.20.99 instead of the correct 172.16.20.1.
Corrective fix: change laptop0's default gateway from 172.16.20.99 to
172.16.20.1 (either by editing the startup script and re-deploying, or by
running `ip route replace default via 172.16.20.1 dev eth0` live in the
container).
=== END ===
```